SETTINGS (added 15/9/26) ver 44.3
=================

--- MODEL ---
LLM (Ollama)
  The brain. Bigger = smarter, slower, more RAM.
  On i3 / 8GB:     qwen2.5:1.5b-instruct  or  3b-instruct
  On Ryzen 7:  qwen2.5:7b-instruct-q4_K_M


Fallback LLM
  ONLY used if the main model errors out.

Embedding model
  Converts text → vectors for semantic search.
  bge-small  = fast, 133 MB, low RAM (KEEP for i3)
  bge-base   = slower, better recall, ~440 MB
  bge-large  = best, needs ~1.3 GB RAM, only worth it on GPU
  all-MiniLM = fastest, English-only, slightly worse than bge-small
  Changing this REQUIRES a "Rebuild index only" click.

Reranker
  Runs AFTER initial retrieval, re-orders the top hits by relevance.
  Adds ~2-4 seconds. Meaningfully improves accuracy on name/number queries.
  "disabled"      = fastest, most hallucination-prone on edge cases
  MiniLM-L-6      = good speed/quality tradeoff (recommended if you have 8GB+ free)
  MiniLM-L-12     = slower, better
  bge-reranker    = best but heavy

Device
  cpu  = safe default
  cuda = uses GPU. 
  If cuda selected but unavailable, silent fallback to CPU added in

FAISS mode
  cpu  = default
  gpu  = requires faiss-gpu installed

--- RETRIEVAL ---
Top-K per method (default 20)
  How many hits each of FAISS + BM25 returns before fusion.
  Higher = better recall, slightly slower, small risk of noise.
  Sweet spot: 15–25.

Final contexts (default 6)
  After fusion + date decay, how many chunks survive into the final list.
  Higher = more material for the LLM, but might include noise.
  Sweet spot: 5–8.

Contexts fed to LLM (default 8)
  Of the final contexts, how many actually go into the prompt.
  If your answers feel "thin" or miss variants (like MCA morning/evening),
  raise this to 10. Costs speed.

Date half-life (days, default 180)
  How aggressively old documents get down-weighted.
  LOWER = very aggressive (only recent months matter)
  HIGHER = more lenient (older docs still considered)
  180  = 6 months. Good for a live uni site.
  365  = 1 year. If you see many stale answers, LOWER this to 120.
  3650 = 10 years. Never do this — you'll get 2017 answers again.

Section boost (default 1.4)
  When the frontend passes a section, matching chunks get this multiplier.
  1.0 = no boost. 2.0 = strong push. Keep at 1.4 unless retriever
  returns wrong-section chunks.

--- GENERATION ---
Response mode
  short    = 1-3 sentences.
  balanced = one short paragraph. (default)
  detailed = structured bullets. Use when you want comprehensive answers.

Persona / tone
  friendly     = warm, like a senior student. (recommended)
  professional = formal, no small talk.
  concise      = extremely short.
  chill        = casual.

Temperature (default 0.2)
  0    = always picks the safest word. Very repetitive but factual.
  0.2  = slight variation. Good default for factual Q&A.
  0.5  = noticeable variation. Might drift.
  1.0  = creative, hallucination-prone. NEVER use for a helpdesk.

Max tokens (default 700)
  Upper bound on answer length in output tokens.
  700  ≈ 500 words. Enough for most answers.
  Raise to 1200 if you use "detailed" mode.

--- REFRESH BEHAVIOUR ---
Enable latest fetch
  No (default) = fast. Uses the existing index.
  Yes = re-crawls the entire site BEFORE EVERY QUESTION.
        Only useful during active development. 5+ minutes per query.

Refresh data now (button)
  Full pipeline: crawl → extract → chunk → embed → build index.
  Use when the DCSA website actually got new notices.

Rebuild index only (button)
  Skips crawling. Uses already-downloaded files.
  Use when you changed the embedding model, chunksize, or want a
  fresh index without re-downloading 900+ pages.

